The Security Council,

Reaffirming its full commitment to the peace process in the Republic of Colombia,

Recalling all its resolutions and presidential and press statements regarding the peace process in Colombia,

Recalling in particular resolution 2366 (2017), which established the United Nations Verification Mission in Colombia (the Verification Mission), and resolutions 2574 (2021), 2673 (2023), 2694 (2023) and 2754 (2024),

Taking note of the report of the Secretary-General on the United Nations Verification Mission in Colombia (S/2025/595), and welcoming the progress reported therein,

Welcoming the progress made towards peace across Colombia since the adoption of the 2016 Final Peace Agreement for Ending the Conflict and Building a Stable and Lasting Peace (the Final Agreement), and urging the parties, with the support of relevant State institutions and security forces, as well as civil society, including women and youth, to continue building upon this progress and to address ongoing challenges through comprehensive implementation of the Final Agreement,

Expressing grave concern at the continuing violence in conflict-affected areas, including in Catatumbo, and at its humanitarian impact, including displacement, confinement, and the recruitment and use of children,

Condemning all attacks against civilians, former combatants, human rights defenders, social leaders, and members of the security forces, and stressing the need to bring perpetrators to justice,

Emphasizing the disproportionate impact of the conflict on women, children, Indigenous and Afro-Colombian peoples, persons with disabilities, and other vulnerable groups,

Reaffirming the importance of full, equal, meaningful and safe participation of women in all stages of peace processes, and of integrating a gender perspective as a cross-cutting issue into implementation, verification and reporting,

Recognizing the contribution of the Verification Mission to building confidence in the Comprehensive System for Truth, Justice, Reparation and Non-Repetition established under the Final Agreement, including through verification of compliance with sentences issued by the Special Jurisdiction for Peace,

Stressing the importance of comprehensive rural reform and implementation of the Ethnic Chapter, and of strengthening State presence and civilian institutions in conflict-affected territories,

Acknowledging the efforts of the Government of Colombia to seek broader peace through dialogue, and recognizing that ceasefire agreements are a step towards the development of comprehensive peace agreements,

Reiterating its willingness to consider mandating the Verification Mission to monitor and verify the implementation of a ceasefire agreement between the Government of Colombia and armed groups engaged in peace negotiations when the Secretary-General confirms that conditions, including a ceasefire with appropriate verification protocols, have been reached,

Recalling the importance of countering illicit drugs, including through crop substitution and alternative development, and of addressing the structural factors underlying the conflict,

Reaffirming its commitment to the sovereignty, independence, unity and territorial integrity of Colombia,

Reaffirming the obligations of all parties under international humanitarian law and international human rights law,

Welcoming the role of the Special Representative of the Secretary-General in supporting the peace process and political dialogues,

1. Decides to extend the mandate of the United Nations Verification Mission in Colombia, as set out in resolution 2366 (2017) and subsequently expanded by resolutions 2574 (2021), 2673 (2023), 2694 (2023) and 2754 (2024), until 31 October 2026;

2. Reaffirms its full support for the comprehensive implementation of the Final Agreement, including rural reform, the Ethnic Chapter, gender provisions, transitional justice, reincorporation, and comprehensive security guarantees for former combatants and communities;

3. Calls upon all parties to respect the Final Agreement and to ensure the safety and security of former combatants, their families, and communities;

4. Calls for the immediate cessation of all acts of violence against civilians, including attacks against human rights defenders, social leaders, women, children, Indigenous and Afro-Colombian peoples, and former combatants;

5. Urges the Government of Colombia to continue strengthening State presence and civilian institutions in conflict-affected areas, to take necessary measures for the protection of civilians, and to combat impunity;

6. Encourages the Government of Colombia to continue to address illicit drugs and related criminal economies through comprehensive and human rights-respecting approaches, including crop substitution and alternative development;

7. Calls upon the Government of Colombia and all relevant actors to ensure the safety, security and unimpeded access of the Verification Mission and its personnel;

8. Requests the Secretary-General to continue to report to the Council every 90 days on the implementation of this resolution and on the work of the Verification Mission, including on progress and challenges related to rural reform, the Ethnic Chapter, verification of sentences issued by the Special Jurisdiction for Peace, protection of children, and the full, equal, meaningful and safe participation of women, and to ensure adequate gender expertise in the Mission’s work;

9. Expresses its willingness to work with the Government of Colombia on the further extension of the mandate of the Verification Mission on the basis of agreement between the parties;

10. Decides to remain actively seized of the matter.

